==Aftermath==
The Georgia Legislature, then controlled by Democrats, changed the state's laws requiring a run-off election only if the winning candidate received less than 45% of the vote. In the [[1996 United States Senate election in Georgia|1996 Senate election]], the winner, Democrat [[Max Cleland]] won with only 48.9% (1.4% ahead of Republican [[Guy Millner]]) thus avoiding a run-off. In 2005 after Republicans took control of the legislature, the run-off requirement was changed back to 50%.<ref name=Runoff_laws>{{cite news |url=https://www.ajc.com/news/local/runoff-system-southern-relic/Ifbyj1S5WTqsAEpGdQJrqL/ |title=Runoff system a Southern relic |date=August 10, 2012 |newspaper=The Atlanta Journal-Constitution |access-date=September 26, 2020}}</ref>

The result of this election would later repeat in reverse in the [[2020–21 United States Senate election in Georgia|2020-2021]] regular Senate election in Georgia, with Republican [[David Perdue]] winning the first round but falling less than one percent below the 50% threshold required to avoid a runoff, and then being defeated in the runoff by Democrat [[Jon Ossoff]] with a similar one point margin.<ref>{{Cite web |url= https://www.npr.org/2021/01/06/952417689/democrat-jon-ossoff-claims-victory-over-david-perdue-in-georgia-runoff|title=Jon Ossoff Wins Georgia Runoff, Handing Democrats Senate Control|website=[[National Public Radio]]|date=6 January 2021|language=en|access-date=9 January 2022}}</ref>

